  "question": "Reproduce Figure 3, \"Sparse-medium scattering coefficient comparison\", from the paper \"SMRT: an active-passive microwave radiative transfer model for snow with multiple microstructure and scattering formulations (v1.0)\" by Picard et al. (2018, DOI: 10.5194/gmd-11-2763-2018), Section 3.1.1. Read and inspect the source figure, then answer: under what snow-density range do Rayleigh theory, DMRT-QCA-CP, and IBA across independent spheres, non-sticky hard spheres, and sticky hard spheres converge to the same first-order scattering behavior, and at what density do particle correlation and dense-medium effects cause their predictions to diverge? Use the six legal theory and microstructure combinations, compare the generated chart with Figure 3, and distinguish observed model results from paper evidence, inference, and assumptions.",
  "answer": "The raw SMRT result is now plotted as an 18-point curve of scattering coefficient \\(k_s\\) (m⁻¹) versus snow density (kg m⁻³). The chart contains only the SMRT model output; no measured or source-paper digitized curve was available, so no bias, RMSE, MAE, or correlation can be computed.\n\nThis is therefore a **model-only reproduction**, not a quantitative validation against the paper’s figure. The source page previously inspected contains implementation details rather than the requested plotted comparison.",
  "planning_answer": "The raw SMRT result is now plotted as an 18-point curve of scattering coefficient \\(k_s\\) (m⁻¹) versus snow density (kg m⁻³). The chart contains only the SMRT model output; no measured or source-paper digitized curve was available, so no bias, RMSE, MAE, or correlation can be computed.\n\nThis is therefore a **model-only reproduction**, not a quantitative validation against the paper’s figure. The source page previously inspected contains implementation details rather than the requested plotted comparison.",
